statement:
Let P ∈ ℕ, let S ⊆ ℝ^P be any set, and let σ < 1 be a real number such that S ∩ B_σ(0) is infinite (|·| the Euclidean norm, B_σ(0) the open ball). Then there exist x ∈ ℝ^P, real numbers λ_k (k ∈ ℕ), points z_k ∈ ℝ^P (k ∈ ℕ) and z ∈ ℝ^P with the following properties:
- |x| < 1;
- λ_k > 0 for every k;
- (λ_k) is strictly decreasing and λ_k → 0;
- λ_k < 1 and |x| ≤ 1 − λ_k for every k;
- x + λ_k z_k ∈ S for every k;
- |z_k| = 1/2 for every k;
- z_k → z and |z| = 1/2.
